  "methodology": "Original method: premium = (defensive ETF Close - Nasdaq Close) / Nasdaq Close * 100; thresholds +/-3% and +/-5%; start in the defensive ETF; switch only to the opposite ETF. Defensive means XUT (utilities) in the pilot and XST (consumer staples) in the reference. Earn each day's return in the previous holding, then execute at that day's close. Initial capital 10,000 in each pair's own currency; fractional shares; unrebalanced 50/50 benchmark. No dividends, fees, slippage or taxes. Signals and executions at the same close are optimistic and not guaranteed executable.",
  "limitations": "Raw share-price differences are not valuations. Share denominations and splits can dominate signals; Yahoo Close is not dividend-adjusted but is normally split-restated, not historical as-traded prices. No rebasing or reconstruction of as-traded prices is used. Zero-switch results are retained. Both XUT/XQQ and XST/XQQ use CAD prices, without FX conversion. XUT is a utilities sector fund, not a like-for-like consumer staples replacement. Correlations use daily returns, not price levels. Historical performance cannot establish the best future investment. XUT replaces the earlier XLP pilot; selecting a new fund after seeing XLP's results introduces selection bias. IYK, KXI, FSTA and VDC remain deferred.",
